  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to use an iterative optimization approach that leverages the structure of the AC1 upper bound function to find a sequence that minimizes it. We start with the parent sequence (height_sequence_1) and iteratively apply operations that reduce the score. The key steps are:\n- Perform a linear programming (LP) step to find a direction that decreases the bound while maintaining feasibility\n- Apply coordinate-wise optimization to refine the solution\n- Use dimension extension to escape local optima by considering other sequence lengths\n- Final polishing with coordinate and pair optimizations to refine the solution\n\nThis approach improves upon the previous solution by making the optimization more targeted and efficient. The bottleneck is the non-convex nature of the problem, which requires a balance between exploration and exploitation.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: The sequence is represented as a vector of non-negative real numbers of fixed length.\n- Variables: Each element of the sequence f \u2208 [0,1000] satisfies \u2211f_i \u2265 0.01\n- Exact Objective: 2n max(convolve(f,f)) / (\u2211f_i)^2\n- Constraints: \n  - Non-negativity: f_i \u2265 0\n  - Upper bound: f_i \u2264 1000\n- Formulation:\n  I. Linear Programming Step:\n      Maximize \u2211g_j subject to:\n        \u2225f * g\u2225_\u221e \u2264 M (where M is the current max convolution)\n        g \u2265 0\n  II. Update: f \u2190 (1-t)f + t g (with adaptive t)\n  III. Coordinate Optimization: For each coordinate, perform local optimization using the influence vector from current convolution\n  IV. Polishing: Combine coordinate and pair optimizations\n- Assumptions: \n  - The problem remains well-posed under normalization\n  - The LP solution provides meaningful update direction\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n- Step 1: Initialization\n  - Read parent sequence if available (N=384)\n  - Create fallback (uniform sequence)\n- Step 2: LP Update\n  - Compute M = max(convolution(parent))\n  - Build constraint matrix (Toeplitz structure)\n  - Solve LP using 'highs' method from scipy.optimize.linprog\n  - Update sequence with optimal solution\n- Step 3: Coordinate Descent\n  - For each coordinate:\n    * Compute influence vector from current convolution\n    * Perform 1D search along coordinate direction\n    * Update coordinate if improvement is found\n- Step 4: Pair Optimization\n  - Randomly select coordinates and apply 2D perturbations\n  - Keep track of improvements with finer granularity\n- Step 5: Dimension Extension\n  - Test sequences of lengths [256, 512] \n  - Use interpolation from best known solution\n- Step 6: Final Optimization\n  - Apply intensive polishing with coordinate descent\n  - Verify final solution with exact evaluator\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\n- Default Configuration:\n  - Parent sequence initialization (N=384)\n  - Bayesian optimization tuning\n  - Step size optimization (initial t=0.2)\n  - Time budget: 60% LP, 30% coordinates, 10% extension\n- Suggested Variations:\n  - Sequence smoothing\n  - Different polynomial initializations\n  - Multi-scale optimization\n- Triggered Adjustments:\n  - Time-based LP intensity\n  - Score-based relaxation\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Time Estimation:\n  - LP: ~500ms for small levels\n  - Coordinate descent: ~100ms per coordinate\n  - Dimensions: 767 for N=384, 1555 for N=768\n- Early Termination:\n  - Timeout checks in LP and convolution\n  - Stall detection (no improvement streak)\n- Validation:\n  - Keep separate validator for score comparisons\n  - Track memory usage for large N\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Step 1: Clipping and normalization\n- Step 2: Ensure finite valid score\n- Step 3: Compare improvements with fallback if needed\n- Step 4: Final score verification\n- Return Format: List of floats with sum \u2265 0.01, values \u2208 [0,1000]\n- Score Acceptance: Strict inequality in comparisons (1e-13 tolerance)\n- Fallback Construction: Interpolate best known solution from other dimensions\n\nThe algorithm is implemented with time constraints in mind, using optimization tricks like:\n- Early termination for stalled progress\n- Staged budget allocation (more time to LP than coordinates)\n- Adaptive step size optimization\n- Progressive refinement of solutions",
